From cc8b3135a073791e89dfb44a8ac790b8c6230d85 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:53:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(botverification): third-party bot verification per
 core.telegram.org/api/bots/verification

Implements the second, independent verification mechanism: a verifier bot marks a
peer with its OWN custom-emoji icon and description, which official clients render
before the name and in the profile. It shares no state with the official badge
(0152/0153): different tables, different rights, different TL fields.

Protocol, all against the layer 228 snapshot the server pins:
  botVerification#f93cd45c bot_id:long icon:long description:string
  botVerifierSettings#b0cd6617 flags:# can_modify_custom_description:flags.1?true
      icon:long company:string custom_description:flags.0?string
  bots.setCustomVerification#8b89dfbd ... = Bool
  user#b1b8cc83        bot_verification_icon:flags2.14?long
  channel#d49f34c6     bot_verification_icon:flags2.13?long
  userFull#6cbe645     bot_verification:flags2.12?BotVerification
  channelFull#a04e8d3a bot_verification:flags2.17?BotVerification
  chatInvite#5c9d3702  bot_verification:flags.13?BotVerification
  botInfo#4d8a0299     verifier_settings:flags.9?BotVerifierSettings

bots.setCustomVerification stops being a BOT_VERIFIER_FORBIDDEN stub and becomes
the real grant/revoke path for a verifier bot or its owner. Every peer surface
that can carry the mark now carries it, resolved in one batched read on the
serialisation path rather than per peer, and the flag bits are pinned by tests
that XOR the encoded flags word before and after the mark exists -- a wrong bit is
a wrong badge, and a wrong badge is an invisible one.

The icon is a custom emoji document. Granting verifier status or cataloguing an
icon verifies the document actually resolves, because an id no client can fetch
renders as nothing at all: the most likely way to ship a feature that looks
complete server-side and shows nothing in the client.

Verifier status is operator-granted (bot_verifier_settings, projected as
botInfo.verifier_settings) with a kill switch that immediately stops the marks of
that verifier from projecting. Approving an application grants the mark in the same
transaction as the status change, so an approved application without its mark
cannot exist. Built-in @verifierbot (0155) files applications and reports
decisions; it is deliberately NOT officially verified, so the two mechanisms stay
visually and semantically distinct.

Admin panel gains a Third-party verification section -- application queue,
verifiers, icon catalogue and granted marks -- behind two new permissions
(botverification.review, botverification.manage), separate from verification.review.
